1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,482
The conditions that cause a production
incident — a node failure, a bad deploy,

2
00:00:07,482 --> 00:00:12,078
memory exhaustion — are the same conditions
that kill the agent responding to it.

3
00:00:12,078 --> 00:00:16,143
And an agent that holds its state in
memory doesn't degrade gracefully.

4
00:00:16,143 --> 00:00:17,404
It stops.

5
00:00:17,404 --> 00:00:20,080
Then a human restarts
the incident from zero,

6
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:23,112
with no idea which remediation
actions already ran.

7
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:30,793
Continuum is an incident-response agent whose memory
lives in CockroachDB instead of in the process.

8
00:00:30,793 --> 00:00:34,681
It runs on AWS Lambda with
no provisioned concurrency,

9
00:00:34,681 --> 00:00:36,985
so every invocation starts cold.

10
00:00:36,985 --> 00:00:39,476
Which means the process is allowed to die.

11
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,595
Five agents, one write path.

12
00:00:42,595 --> 00:00:43,911
Before any reasoning,

13
00:00:43,911 --> 00:00:48,798
the orchestrator's first action is always
a recovery read against CockroachDB.

14
00:00:48,798 --> 00:00:52,798
If an incident is already open, it
picks up from the durable state —

15
00:00:52,798 --> 00:00:53,916
never from scratch.

16
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,423
An alert fires.

17
00:00:56,423 --> 00:00:58,104
Bedrock embeds it.

18
00:00:58,104 --> 00:01:01,892
CockroachDB's vector index
finds the closest past incident,

19
00:01:01,892 --> 00:01:04,075
and Claude proposes the next step.

20
00:01:04,075 --> 00:01:07,356
That step is committed as
executing — before it runs.

21
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:09,527
Now watch.

22
00:01:09,527 --> 00:01:11,611
The process is killed mid-step.

23
00:01:11,611 --> 00:01:13,299
No graceful shutdown.

24
00:01:13,299 --> 00:01:14,602
No checkpoint.

25
00:01:14,602 --> 00:01:16,344
Nothing gets a chance to clean up.

26
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:23,162
The process is gone.

27
00:01:23,162 --> 00:01:27,919
The step is still there — sitting in
executing, with nothing alive that owns it.

28
00:01:27,919 --> 00:01:31,212
That row is the agent's memory,
and it outlived the agent.

29
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:37,269
A cold Lambda invocation.

30
00:01:37,269 --> 00:01:42,763
It reads CockroachDB first, finds the
interrupted step, and re-runs that exact step.

31
00:01:42,763 --> 00:01:44,390
Not from scratch.

32
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:45,648
Not skipped.

33
00:01:45,648 --> 00:01:46,816
Not duplicated.

34
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:50,794
That isn't one lucky take.

35
00:01:50,794 --> 00:01:52,955
Fifty interrupted incidents.

36
00:01:52,955 --> 00:01:54,782
Fifty clean resumes.

37
00:01:54,782 --> 00:01:59,515
Zero duplicated actions, zero lost
steps — counted from the durable rows,

38
00:01:59,515 --> 00:02:00,488
not from a log.

39
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:03,897
And it isn't only our own kill switch.

40
00:02:03,897 --> 00:02:07,553
Here, AWS terminates the
function itself, mid-step,

41
00:02:07,553 --> 00:02:10,206
with no signal the process can catch.

42
00:02:10,206 --> 00:02:12,820
All fifteen recovered, exactly once.

43
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,911
The memory layer scales with it.

44
00:02:15,911 --> 00:02:18,483
From one hundred
incidents to ten thousand,

45
00:02:18,483 --> 00:02:23,087
CockroachDB's vector index stays flat
while a full scan climbs away from it —

46
00:02:23,087 --> 00:02:25,000
six times faster at the top end.

47
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:31,268
The same memory is queryable live,
through CockroachDB's managed MCP server —

48
00:02:31,268 --> 00:02:34,392
read-only, called by
the application itself.

49
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:37,699
Type-checked, linted and gated in CI,

50
00:02:37,699 --> 00:02:43,576
with the recovery contract pinned by tests
that hard-kill a real process on every push.

51
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,751
Agents will keep dying mid-task.

52
00:02:46,751 --> 00:02:50,088
Continuum is the one that picks
up exactly where it left off.
